{"name":"Canyon Falls","hex":"#97987f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"canyon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A valley, especially a long, narrow, steep valley, cut in rock by a river."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of water, liquid, or another substance, to flow in or into a canyon."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/canyon"},{"term":"falls","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A waterfall."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of fall"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/falls"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canyon Falls, Kentucky","blocks":[{"text":"Canyon Falls is an unincorporated community in Lee County, Kentucky, United States."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon_Falls%2C_Kentucky"},{"title":"Canyon Falls Bridge","blocks":[{"text":"The Canyon Falls Bridge is a bridge located on US Highway 41 (US 41) over the Sturgeon River in L'Anse Township, Michigan. It was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1999."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_A.jpg/330px-Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon_Falls_Bridge"},{"title":"Canyon Falls (Washington)","blocks":[{"text":"Canyon Falls is the second of the three waterfalls on the South Fork Skykomish River. The falls occur about halfway between Eagle Falls and Sunset Falls."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/00/Canyon_Falls%2C_Skykomish_River%2C_ca_1911_%28PICKETT_72%29.jpeg/330px-Canyon_Falls%2C_Skykomish_River%2C_ca_1911_%28PICKETT_72%29.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon_Falls_(Washington)"},{"title":"Canyon","blocks":[{"text":"A canyon, gorge or chasm, is a deep cleft between escarpments or cliffs resulting from weathering and the erosive activity of a river over geologic time scales. Rivers have a natural tendency to cut through underlying surfaces, eventually wearing away rock layers as sediments are removed downstream. A river bed will gradually reach a baseline elevation, which is the same elevation as the body of water into which the river drains. The processes of weathering and erosion will form canyons when the river's headwaters and estuary are at significantly different elevations, particularly through regions where softer rock layers are intermingled with harder layers more resistant to weathering."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg/330px-Aerial_view_of_canyons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_C.jpg/120px-Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_C.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_C.jpg/250px-Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_C.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_C.jpg/120px-Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_C.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_C.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_B.jpg/120px-Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_B.jpg/250px-Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_B.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Canyon_Falls_Bridge_MI_B.